<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
		lunarlog is a local-first cycle tracker with an optional account. Two
		parties receive data off-device, both only for app functionality:

		- Supabase (account sign-in and cross-device sync): the account email
		  and the cycle entries the operator chooses to sync, protected by
		  TLS in transit and Supabase's own AES-256 at-rest infrastructure
		  encryption (not an app-managed cipher — PRIVACY.md Section 6).
		  Both are linked to the account (Email Address, Health).
		- Sentry (crash reporting via the sentry_flutter SDK): crash and error
		  events reduced to an allowlist before upload (no user, device name,
		  request query, header, body, note, tag, date, or profile name); see
		  lib/observability/scrub.dart. Not linked to identity (Crash Data).

		No tracking, no advertising, no analytics beyond crash reports.

		Issue #265: required-reason API usage from the CocoaPod-delivered
		Flutter plugins (e.g., flutter_secure_storage, shared_preferences,
		local_auth, sentry_flutter) is declared in their own privacy
		manifests, but pubspec.yaml's `sqlite3` dependency (no
		`sqlite3_flutter_libs`, no ios/Podfile) is NOT one of those; its
		Dart native-build hook downloads a precompiled `libsqlite3` dynamic
		library from github.com/simolus3/sqlite3.dart's releases (the
		`source: null` default branch of `SqliteBinary.forBuild` in the
		package's own `hook/build.dart`/`lib/src/hook/compile/description.dart`,
		confirmed by reading that source at pinned version 3.5.2) and bundles
		it into Runner.app with no privacy manifest of its own, so any
		required-reason API it calls must be declared HERE instead. Audited
		against Apple's "Describing use of required reason API" documentation
		(developer.apple.com/documentation/bundleresources/describing-use-of-required-reason-api)
		and cross-checked against multiple independent sources reproducing
		its reason-code table, since that page is not fetchable as plain
		text from this environment:
		- File Timestamp (`NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp`,
		  reason C617.1, file metadata such as timestamps/size for files
		  within the app container): SQLite's unix VFS (`os_unix.c`,
		  `unixFileSize`) calls `fstat()` to read the database file's size
		  on every open, a well-documented reason app binaries bundling
		  SQLite need this category declared.
		- User Defaults (`NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults`):
		  AppDelegate.swift's `HealthKitChannelHandler` reads/writes
		  `UserDefaults.standard` directly (the `boundProfileId` guard
		  state, issue #173) — reason CA92.1 (app-exclusive access, not
		  an App Group); issue #141 adds reason CA92.2 (information
		  shared between an app extension and its containing app) for
		  the App Group suite the Dart side writes the home-screen
		  widget's discreet render payload into
		  (`lib/domain/widget/widget_cycle_state.dart` documents the
		  exact, minimal field set; the widget extension's own manifest,
		  ios/LunarLogWidget/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy, declares CA92.2 too).
		- NOT declared: System Boot Time, Disk Space, Active Keyboards.
		  No first-party code (AppDelegate.swift, including the HealthKit
		  and notification handling) or Flutter/Dart code calls any of
		  these, and the default SQLite build here (no custom VFS, no
		  disk-space or low-space handling compiled in via the defines in
		  the package's `hook/build.dart`) gives no reason to expect them
		  either; but this was reasoned from source, not from Xcode's own
		  "Generate Privacy Report" static analysis of the actual linked
		  binary (unavailable from this non-macOS environment; there is no
		  documented xcodebuild-only equivalent; see
		  docs/ops/ios-privacy-manifest.md for the operator follow-up this
		  leaves open).
		flutter_local_notifications and FirebaseMessaging (both referenced
		in AppDelegate.swift's comments) are CocoaPods and are assumed
		covered by their own manifests per the existing "Flutter plugins"
		note above; unaudited here, out of issue #265's scope.

		Issue #156 added the com.apple.developer.healthkit entitlement; #173
		landed the first-party HealthKit adapter, #193/#374 the opt-in,
		forward-only menstrual-flow writes (the bound profile's user-logged
		flow and spotting records, written into the OS HealthKit store on
		this device), and #217 the user-initiated menstrual-flow import
		(#781's owner decision reversing the earlier write-only posture).
		The import reads only menstrualFlow, only on an explicit Settings
		action, only for the bound profile, and over the whole available
		history (paged, issue #992; no longer a bounded 30-day window);
		samples this app itself wrote are excluded by source
		filtering, and Apple's four computed cycle-deviation types are never
		requested, read, or written. The import keeps data on the device:
		the app itself never transmits HealthKit data off-device and no
		third party receives it through this feature, so neither direction
		is a new *collected* data type for App Store Connect purposes and
		the collected-data-types list below is unchanged. The "Health"
		entry above is the app's own cycle/symptom data synced to Supabase
		under the operator's separate, optional account sync, unrelated
		to HealthKit. A clinical export handed to the OS share sheet (the
		JSON / FHIR files from Settings -> Your data) is a user-initiated
		transfer to a destination the user chooses, not app collection.
		HealthKit writes are user-logged data only: never a predicted or
		derived cycle value (App Review guideline 5.1.3; the written rule
		lives in lib/data/health/health_channel.dart's library doc). Issue
		#254 recorded this reconciliation; App Store Connect's App Privacy
		answers — including the new read/import capability (issue #21) —
		must still be re-verified against this manifest before the
		next submission that includes HealthKit.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeHealth</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeCrashData</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
				<string>CA92.2</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
